.TH ZGET 1 "August 19, 2026" "zget @PROJECT_VERSION@" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
zget \- fetch one member from a remote ZIP archive
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B zget
.RB [ \-o
.IR FILE ]
.I URL MEMBER
.br
.B zget
.B \-l
.I URL
.RI [ MEMBER ]
.br
.B zget
.B \-1
.I URL
.RI [ MEMBER ]
.br
.B zget
.RB [ \-h | \-\-help ]
.br
.B zget
.B \-\-version
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B zget
fetches one exact, case-sensitive member from a remote ZIP or ZIP64 archive
without downloading the entire archive.
It requires HTTP Range support and does not fall back to a full download.
.PP
The Central Directory is parsed as an HTTP stream. Metadata for each
non-matching entry is discarded immediately, so memory use is independent of
the archive entry count. The Central Directory transfer stops when the first
exact name match is found. The member payload is then streamed through STORE
or raw-DEFLATE decoding and checked against its ZIP CRC32.
.PP
For extraction, both a
.I URL
and an exact
.I MEMBER
are required; a URL by itself does not request an archive listing.
.PP
By default, member data is written to standard output. When
.B \-o
is used, output is first written to a temporary file in the destination
directory. The destination is published only after extraction and CRC
validation succeed, and an existing path is never overwritten.
.PP
.B \-l
streams a listing directly from the Central Directory. Each output row contains
the uncompressed length, ZIP-local modification date and time, and member name
in a layout inspired by
.BR unzip (1).
ZIP timestamps have no timezone; invalid packed timestamps are shown as dashes.
Control characters, backslashes, and legacy non-UTF-8 bytes are escaped so an
archive name cannot create additional terminal lines. Listing memory use is
independent of the entry count.
.PP
.B \-1
uses the same streaming lookup but writes only member names, one per line,
without a header or totals. Names receive the same safe escaping as the table
listing. This form follows the convention established by
.BR zipinfo (1).
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-o " FILE"
Write the member to
.I FILE
instead of standard output.
.TP
.B \-l
List archive members to standard output. With
.IR MEMBER ,
emit the first exact match and stop; otherwise emit every member. A URL without
this explicit option does not imply listing.
.TP
.B \-1
List only archive member names, one per line. With
.IR MEMBER ,
emit the first exact match and stop; otherwise emit every member.
.TP
.BR \-h , " " \-\-help
Show command usage and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Show the zget version and exit.
.SH HTTP REQUIREMENTS
Each archive request must return a matching 206 Partial Content response with
an identity Content-Encoding and the expected Content-Range and body length.
HTTPS redirects may not downgrade to HTTP. A strong ETag, when supplied by the
server, is used with subsequent requests to ensure that all ranges refer to the
same archive version.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The requested extraction or listing completed successfully.
.TP
.B 1
An HTTP, ZIP, integrity, compression, output, or resource-limit error occurred.
.TP
.B 2
The command line was invalid.
.SH EXAMPLES
Write a member to standard output:
.PP
.nf
zget https://example.com/archive.zip README.txt
.fi
.PP
Publish a validated member as a local file:
.PP
.nf
zget -o report.pdf https://example.com/documents.zip path/to/report.pdf
.fi
.PP
Process a member as a stream:
.PP
.nf
zget https://example.com/data.zip config.json | jq .
.fi
.PP
Stream the archive listing:
.PP
.nf
zget -l https://example.com/archive.zip
.fi
.PP
List one exact member without extracting it:
.PP
.nf
zget -l https://example.com/documents.zip path/to/report.pdf
.fi
.PP
List only member names:
.PP
.nf
zget -1 https://example.com/archive.zip
.fi
.SH LIMITATIONS
Only single-volume ZIP32 and ZIP64 archives are supported. Member names are
exact full paths; there is no path normalization or globbing. Compression
methods are limited to STORE and DEFLATE. Encryption and split archives are
not supported.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR curl (1),
.BR unzip (1),
.BR zipinfo (1)
